✴️ Day 5 of #90DaysOfDevOps
✔️  Today i learned about a runbook, what is runbook, why its important & how to create a standard runbook 
Runbook is a step-by-step troubleshooting or operational guide that helps you solve the some issue quickly in future.

✔️ Below commands used for health check & troubleshooting 

✔️ cpu monitoring :

htop : for CPU consuming process
mpstat : show the CPU stat per core

✔️ memory monitoring :

free -m : show ram uses in mb

✔️ disk managnment process :

df -h : show filesystrem uses
du -sh/var/log/* : find large file / dirctories

✔️ network commands :

ss -tulnp : show listning ports & services
t : TCP, U : UDP, l : listening, n : numeric, p : process

✔️ Service managment :

sudo systemctl status nginx : check the service status
journalctl -u nginx -n 50 : view the service logs
u : unit / service, n : last 50 lines
tail -f /var/log//nginx/error.log : watch the logs in real time

✔️ Process Commands :

ps aux : list all process
kill -9 PID : force stop process

✔️ Why runbook matters in DevOps :

1) Faster recovery

2) Reduced downtime

3) Team collaboration

4) Knowledge sharing
